CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ nested: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ returned: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ temporary: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ new-expression: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
double delete: FAILURE$
dynamic object.*: FAILURE$
--
N5008 [dcl.init.aggr]/4.2, [dcl.init.general]/16.6: in `A a{owner(5), 1}'
the element a.o is copy-initialized from the temporary owner(5) by owner's
constructor.  The front end built a struct value from the temporary's
bits and then destroyed the temporary, so a.o pointed to freed memory:
spurious use-after-free and double-free failures (bin53; the same for
`P p{"ab", 1}' with a std::string member).  Elements of a non-POD class
type of an aggregate without bases are now constructed in the object, or
in the temporary for `A{...}' (aggregate_member_initialization), the
others assigned.  g++ 13 (with -fsanitize=address) and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
